e: emit lua from the schema, parse\n- `tools/typeeager.lua` — typeeager — the BAND-LOCAL TYPE-INFERENCE eager-vs-deferred probe (federation,\n- `tools/varintcol.lua` — varintcol — the aggressive-varint-columnar compaction ceiling ([[cartograph-thin-index]]).\n","after":"# Before you do it by hand: question → instrument\n\n**The standing rule** (user, 2026-09-26): *finding what cartograph can do for us is a standing rule, and every\ndiscovery about its capabilities is written down, so nobody does it by hand the next morning.*\n\nThree habits make that true:\n\n1. **Look here first.** Before writing a throwaway script, find the question below. Many of the tools are\n   phrased as the question they answer (their first header line).\n2. **A throwaway that answers a question twice becomes a tool** — or goes under\n   [Done by hand, not yet a tool](#done-by-hand-not-yet-a-tool) with its ticket, so the next person knows it\n   was needed and what it found.\n3. **Every new tool or flag gets its line here in the same commit.** `tests/questions_index_spec.lua` fails when a\n   `tools/*.lua` appears nowhere in this file — the older capability inventory (a memory file) was a rule alone\n   and stopped being updated for a month; this one is fenced.\n\nAll commands run from the repo root as `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/<tool>.lua …`; the argument lists below are\nthe tools' own usage lines. Corpus names come from `tools/corpora.lua`.\n\n## Is the graph right? Did something drift?\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Do all corpora still produce their pinned counts / the saved graph, and do the oracles agree? | `tools/matrix.lua [<corpus>...] [--cols counts,struct,dfpar,fold,silent,cache,par] [--save] [--jobs N]` |\n| One corpus against its pin and baseline | `tools/gate.lua <corpus> [--save]` |\n| WHAT KIND of node moved vs the baseline (a grammar upgrade reading locals as functions shows as `+ function bare`) | `tools/gate.lua <corpus> --kinds` (graphdiff.by_kind) |\n| Did the DF distribution move? (a derived projection counts/struct never compare) | `tools/matrix.lua <corpus> --cols dfshape` — vs the distribution the baseline recorded at `--save` |\n| Does the expression IR read what dataflow reads (the two-implementation self-gate)? | `tools/exprcensus.lua <dir> [--lang <l>] [--show <class>]` — class `match` = a match test the IR cannot see from one row (named, not a defect) |\n| What did extraction SEE and not RECORD? Which constructs leave no fact in the graph (the L2 loss)? Is an absence \"the code does not do that\" or \"the schema had no slot\"? | `tools/lossreport.lua <corpus|dir> [--lang L] [--top N] [--show TYPE]` — maximal dark subtrees by type and position, staged (extraction vs after the post-passes), a WITNESS line that must decline calls. ejabberd/src: -spec 3391, record constructs, macro args, data tuples, exports; converse.js: imports (no site on import edges), TS declarations |\n| Which inferred edges does the code contradict (fabrication)? | `tools/fabcensus.lua <corpus|path> [--show <bucket>]` |\n| REVERSE: which REFUSED calls could the calling file's own import/require binding settle? (a work list) | `tools/fabcensus.lua <root> --backward` — buckets settles / settles-by-shape / several / via-reexport / bound-lacks |\n| Which files USE another file's export (or global) with no require of it — working by load order? (a work list) | `tools/userequire.lua <corpus>`; lint rule `use-without-require` (info, suggestive) |\n| Which captures and query slots does each language bind, and which slots could a language fill that it does not (PROJECTING)? | `tools/specaudit.lua --capabilities [--top=N]` (no corpus needed; rediscovers CART-0692) |\n| Same corpus, same graph twice? (per layer) | `tools/determinism.lua <corpus|dir> [--runs N]` |\n| Does a data reader agree with an independent implementation, row by row? | `tools/oraclejoin.lua <join> [--repos a,b,…] [--show N]` |\n| Does a GENERATOR declaration read what the hand-rolled reader it re-expresses reads? (metaprogramming as one relation) | `tools/generatorjoin.lua erlreg <root> \\| erlderive <root> \\| ruby <corpus> \\| rails <corpus> [--show N]` — rows keyed by site, both directions, refusals by reason. ejabberd erlreg 26/26; ruby.attr discourse 371/371, activesupport 159 equal + 40 where the ORIGINAL ignores the receiver; rails.dsl discourse 1276/1276 |\n| Does a module compare node types its declared languages lack? (the language fence) | `tools/langaudit.lua [--all]` |\n| What can this tree NOT be falsified by? Which promises has any test triggered? | `tools/instrumentcensus.lua lines` · `tools/refusalcensus.lua /tmp/cov.txt` |\n| Construct the input that breaks a promise instead of searching for one | `tools/counterexample.lua [--keep]` |\n| Can cartograph refactor itself and stay green? | `tools/selfrefactor.lua [--dir D] [--n N]` |\n\n## The grammar\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| What tree does tree-sitter really build (field names, anonymous tokens)? Did a grammar upgrade change it? | `tools/tsdump.lua <lang> <file|-> [--named] [--view] [--parser <so>] [--vs <so>]` (bob keeps the previous nvim's parsers for `--vs`) |\n| Does the parse view (lua dialect, scheme `@`, cpp `= default`) fix a misread? | `tools/tsdump.lua <lang> <file> --view` |\n| \"Walk a corpus and count that\" without writing a file | `tools/probe.lua <corpus|dir> --expr '<chunk>'` |\n\n## Is it already here? (ask BEFORE implementing something or searching elsewhere)\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Does the tree already contain this functionality, compiled out by default? How is it turned on? (erlang + rebar + autoconf) | `tools/features.lua <root> [--all]` — configure switch and default -> rebar variable -> macro / dependency -> the `-ifdef` code it compiles out, with line ranges and functions. ejabberd: SIP (~1,400 lines, `--enable-sip`), Elixir, roster gateway workaround, multihost SQL schema |\n| Is a missing dependency or behaviour producer really missing, or just behind a disabled feature? | `tools/producers.lua <root>` marks each one `[compiled out by default: MACRO — ./configure --enable-x]` |\n\n## What does the tree reach for? (environment, dependencies)\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| What does a vendored artifact reach for, and does anything supply it? | `tools/surface.lua` |\n| Distil a surface into an L2 environment profile | `tools/nodedistill.lua`, `tools/npmdistill.lua <corpus|dir>`, `tools/domdistill.lua`, `tools/erldistill.lua` (now also OTP behaviour callbacks), `tools/hrldistill.lua --from <dir-or-.hrl>`, `tools/dtsread.lua`, `tools/nvimdistill.lua [--show]` (the `nvim` profile from the running Neovim runtime's own LuaLS annotations — declared returns, e.g. `vim.fn.system` -> string; activated by the nvim-plugin / nvim-config shapes; shares `cartograph.metaread` with luadistill) |\n| The Maven build layer, and the java import resolver against package declarations | `tools/pomtree.lua <repo>…`, `tools/javaimports.lua <repo>…` (⚠ `tools/mavenpoms.lua` DOWNLOADS from Maven Central — network) |\n| Does the stage partition carry the JS runner globals? | `tools/stagefit.lua <corpus|dir> [--decl <key>]` |\n| A fact the tree lacks: who CONSUMES it (other repos' manifests), where the dependencies it selects are on disk, who produces the callbacks its -behaviour lines owe — each cited by the selecting line | `tools/producers.lua <root> [--search DIR]... [--lib DIR]...` (CART-1118; finds, attaches nothing) |\n\n## Dead or alive?\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Which functions are dead / possibly dead, and why is this one alive? | lint `dead-confined`, `dead-function` (MCP `lint_run`, `:CartographLint`); the alibi verb (`lint.alibi(store)`, MCP `why`) — inheritance contract both ways since CART-0714; erlang `-behaviour` callbacks (the tree's `-callback` + the otp-api profile's `behaviours`, alibi kind `behaviour-callback`) since CART-1117 |\n| Which records does an erlang tree declare and never use; which fields are never named? | `tools/erlrecordcensus.lua --unused` |\n\n## Across the wire: the XMPP triple (ejabberd × payload spec × converse.js)\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Which client function's IQ request reaches which ejabberd handler CLAUSE, with what bound across the wire? | `tools/xmppmerge.lua [--rows]` |\n| Seen from the server: per handler its namespaces, per clause reached / shadowed / rejected / unasked | `tools/xmppmerge.lua --server-view` |\n| Is the absent-attribute rule still the generated decoder's? (oracle over every `decode_*_attr_*`) | `tools/xmppmerge.lua --check-absent` |\n| Every registered IQ endpoint (both carriers), its handler, and the request each clause ACCEPTS as XML | `tools/xmppserver.lua [<server-root>] --rows` |\n| What each handler SENDS, as a term (complete / partial / one-of several records / opaque) and whether it is on the wire — through case/if joins, patterns, and the callees' clauses (tree + dependency + OTP source) | `tools/xmppserver.lua --sends [--rows] [--otp <OTP src root>]` (default: `~/git/otp_src_<installed version>`) (ejabberd: 124 of 184 sites name one record, was 80; 22 more one of several; all on the wire) |\n| What does the server REPLY to each client request — the handler run with the client's own request term (one of several replies when its arms differ), and how many of the reply's unknowns the request fills | `tools/xmppmerge.lua --responses [--rows] [--otp DIR]`; library `xmppserver.responses(merged, dir, {E, otp})` (CART-1135) |\n| Does the spec-driven ENCODER (record -> XML, `xmppspec.encode`) agree with xmpp's own generated encoder, entry by entry? | `tools/xmppencode.lua [--rows] [--only NAME]` (evaluates each entry's `encode_<name>` from source; CART-1138) |\n| The XMPP WIRE as an algebra grammar (bytes <-> #xmlel <-> record) — do its laws hold, and does it agree with the running fxml byte for byte? | `tools/xmppgrammar.lua [--rows]`; library `xmppgrammar.new{spec, otp}` / `.register(G)` (A.grammar 'xmpp': an embed prints and parses through it), `.read`, `.print_el` (CART-1138) |\n| What does a NIF return (jid's splitter, stringprep) — can a JID the tree reads be known, and do the models agree with the running runtime? | `tools/p1natives.lua [--len N] [--rows]` (exhaustive over the bytes that matter); library `p1natives.models`, handed to `erlterms.program{natives}` (on by default in `xmppserver.program`) — consulted only where the source is absent or a nif_error stub (CART-1138) |\n| Generate a CLIENT of a server from its implementation (every request its handlers accept, every reply they can give) — and check it: contract, standalone, in-process imitation, coverage of the real client | `tools/peergen.lua [--out FILE] [--rows] [--merge] [--twice]` (XMPP IQ), `tools/peergen.lua --grpc ROOT [--client DIR]` (gRPC from .proto: vendored copies merged, drift kept); core `peergen.generate(model)` (protocol-free), adapters `xmpppeer.model(root, {spec, otp})`, `grpcpeer.model(root)` (CART-1138) |\n| Which clause does each erlang call reach — and which clauses does NO call reach (dead clauses), or which calls does no clause take (a function_clause waiting)? | `tools/erlclauses.lua [<root>] [--rows]`; library `erlclauses.census(dir, {E})` (CART-1110) |\n| What the client BUILDS: every `stx` template as an element tree with holes and namespaces | `tools/stxcensus.lua <dir> [--all] [--stanzas] [--json <out>]` |\n| What `xmpp_codec.spec` declares, and its consistency oracles (records, OTP's own parser) | `tools/xmppspeccensus.lua [--rows] [--erl]` |\n| Every record reference against its module's scope, with dependency roots; the compiler as oracle | `tools/erlrecordcensus.lua [--root DIR] [--app NAME=DIR] [--lib DIR] [--rows] [--erl]` |\n| Registered vs declared namespaces, joined by URI (the older CONFORM) | `tools/rootjoin.lua [<left>] [<right>] [--rows]` |\n| Could we emit a client for this endpoint, and if not, who blocks it? | `tools/endpointcensus.lua <corpus|dir> [--rows]` |\n| Who implements and who calls each gRPC method across languages? | `tools/grpcjoin.lua [<corpus>|<dir>]` |\n\n## Templates, clones, the algebra\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Other implementations of one concept, incomplete ones included | `tools/variants.lua <corpus|dir> --query <name>` |\n| What should a template be able to say? What do container members share? | `tools/divergecensus.lua <corpus|path>`, `tools/membercensus.lua <corpus|dir>` |\n| The helper signature of every near pair; does the pairwise lgg compose? | `tools/hocensus.lua`, `tools/familydiff.lua <corpus|dir>` |\n| Which syntactic positions does the minting rule not cover? | `tools/mintcensus.lua <corpus|dir> [--lang L]` |\n| Drive the vendored algebra with real IR; how much of it is absorbed; has the vendored copy drifted? | `tools/algebradrive.lua`, `tools/elemdrive.lua`, `tools/algebraledger.lua`, `tools/vendordrift.lua [--gate]` |\n\n## Performance\n\n| Question | Instrument |\n|---|---|\n| Every shipped performance lens over every function, with its denominator | `tools/perfscan.lua <root> [--files <pat>]` |\n| Redundant idempotent setup, and where one copy would do | `tools/redundancy.lua <root>` |\n| What a file's references resolve to (fact cost); profile-guided index-a-scan | `tools/factcost.lua <file.lua>…`, `tools/idxprofile.lua <module> <file.lua> <workload.lua>` |\n| Does `pattern_degree` agree with Lua's own matcher? | `tools/patternjoin.lua ['<pattern>' ...]` |\n| The runtime tier from outside the process (OpenTelemetry spans) | `tools/otelobserve.lua <corpus|dir> --spans <file>` |\n| What BREAKS if this Lua is transliterated to JavaScript? (the census before any emitter) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/jsbreaks.lua <dir> [--show CLASS|key]` — tree-sitter QUERIES over every .lua file (declarative, every byte covered), each site in a break class by whether an ALWAYS-FAITHFUL template exists (faithful for every runtime type, never \"if the inferred type is right\"): TEMPLATE / PARTIAL (a derivable subset: RegExp-translatable patterns, the `continue` goto) / NONE (load, setfenv, %b/%f, general goto, collectgarbage) / DECISION (depends on a REPRESENTATION choice: strings as bytes or UTF-16, tables as Map / object / array) / HOST (vim.*, io, os, ffi — a declared template per member, or a refusal). Vocabularies come from the running Lua (`string`, `_G`). Controls: calls, definitions, labels. MEASURED 2026-09-29 over lua/ (319 files, 7.6 MB): NONE 77 · DECISION 32665 (t[expr] 16353, # 8131, t[number] 2252, byte-offset string methods 2744, non-ASCII string literals 1678 in 152 files) · PARTIAL 8729 · HOST 1923 (65 roots, 120+ vim.x.y members) · TEMPLATE 47896; files free of NONE/DECISION/HOST: 4 of 319. |\n| TRANSLITERATE a Lua tree to JavaScript and see what breaks (emit, parse, load) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/luajs.lua <lua dir> <out dir> [--run]` — `cartograph.luajs` emits every file as a CommonJS module expanded into the template pack `lua/cartograph/luajs/pack.js` (faithful for every runtime type: truthiness, and/or, arithmetic with string coercion, floored %, %.14g concatenation, byte strings, multiple values, 1-based arrays; tables by SHAPE from `cartograph.tblshape` — ARRAY `$arr` / RECORD `$rec` / MAP `$map`, accesses polymorphic `$idx`/`$set`; a Lua local gets a fresh JS name whenever the name is visible). Three layers: EMIT refusals by name (a general goto, a non-continue label, a multi-valued last entry of a Map constructor), PARSE by node's own parser (`node --check`, must be 0), RUN each module loaded under node, by outcome (ok / LuaBreak = a pack gap reached, named / LuaError / JS error). MEASURED 2026-09-29 over lua/ (321 files, 36 s): 26 refusals in 9 files, 0 parse failures; modules that LOAD: 254 → 266 (metatables: a WeakMap by identity, 5.1 semantics) → 267 (HOST pack: io files as userdata, os dates/env/exit/remove/rename/execute, `debug.getinfo(1, \"S\")` mapped back to the Lua source via LUAJS_SRC_ROOT); → 273 (Lua patterns: LuaJIT's matcher transliterated from C by tools/cjs.lua) → 314 of 326 (the VIM host pack: nvim's OWN pure-Lua runtime — vim/_core/shared, inspect, uri, fs, the module set DERIVED from the pack's requires — transliterated beside the modules and loaded lazily as nvim defers it; vim.uv / fn / json / mpack / system / env / NIL / log / notify as declared templates matched to probed nvim behaviour; the editor surfaces abort by name at the read; a shape is an optimization — a key the shape cannot hold goes to a hidden SIDE Map, never an abort; pcall never swallows a LuaBreak). The rest, every one a named break: loadstring 7 (Lua source built at run time), the editor 4, a script without its job file 1. The SEMANTICS oracle is `tests/luajs_spec.lua` (differential: Lua with the standard print vs the emitted JS under node, byte-identical). CART-1197 |\n| Declare HOW a Lua construct becomes JS as a PAIR OF EXAMPLES (not code) — the local half of the transliterator | `lua/cartograph/luajs/rules.lua` `M.RULES`: `{ lua = 'a + b', js = '$add(a, b)' }` — both sides READ by the lossless reader (`cartograph.algebraread`, the JS side as javascript), every Lua identifier a hole, the left MATCHED on a node's term modulo layout (gaps and comments dropped), the right INSTANTIATED and printed (algebra match / instantiate / cst_print); hole modes `as = { f = 'name' \\| 'raw' \\| 'block' }`; compile refuses a rule that drops a hole. 31 rules (literals, grouping, indexing, the operators, do, return 0/1); the CONTEXT cases (scope, calls, tables, loops, if, goto, number/string values) stay in `cartograph.luajs`. `algebraread.read_tree(src, lang, tree, visit)` gives each node of a caller's tree its term. `tools/luajs.lua` reports `RULES: n declared, m match(es); k never fired` (a rule no source reaches has no witness). Acceptance of the move: all 357 emitted files BYTE-IDENTICAL to the hand emitter; luajs_spec pins every rule firing — which then surfaced CART-1200 (no values returned ONE nil), fixed as a deliberate output change (`return $mv();`, normalized diff otherwise empty). CART-1199 |\n| Check the Lua→JS transliteration on EVERY program up to a size (not a sample) — control flow, value counts, coercions | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/luajsgen.lua <control\\|values\\|coercion> <max size> [corpus dir] [out dir] [min=<n>]` — `cartograph.luajs.gencheck`: every program of the fragment runs under real Lua (luaref) and, transliterated, under node, compared per program. Prints the COUNT per size first, then the FUNNEL (generated → parses → loads → terminates → emitted → compared → diverged, + Lua-rejected programs the emitter translated anyway), a census of the compared programs by token, UNREACHED tokens (the claim is vacuous for them), divergences by class (value · message · crash · hang, smallest first; a failed node batch is bisected to its program), and the CLAIM with its four parameters (\"exhaustive to size N over the productions of CORPUS ∩ fragment F, atoms P\" — never promoted to proven by raising N). MEASURED 2026-09-29 at size 8 over lua/ (every divergence in <out>/diverged.txt): control 114,838 compared / 0 diverged (goto in 758, for in 512; atoms incl. 0), values 53,815 / 0 value divergences (72 message-only: variable names, CART-1207), coercion 352,161 / 20 (all a non-integer pow: V8 vs glibc, one ulp, CART-1208). FIXED along the way, each pinned in luajs_spec: the numeric-for check ($forprep), `return ...` (all the varargs), %.14g exponent threshold + exact ties to even + -0, the compare message, LuaJIT hex floats; probing its gaps: a zero for-step counts UP (the sign bit). Lua-rejected programs the emitter translates: CART-1209. CART-1206 |\n| Derive a GRAMMAR from a corpus and enumerate EVERY tree up to a size (any tree-sitter language) | `require('cartograph.grammargen')`: `derive({ {name, text} }, lang)` reads each file with the lossless reader a