er='<mutated>' spec=<name>_spec [repo=<dir>] [keep=1]` — copies the repo (all but .git) to a scratch root, runs the spec there FIRST (a red baseline or a run of nothing REFUSES), applies the mutation by example (rewrite-by-example: staged, parse-guarded; a mutation that matches nothing REFUSES by name), runs the spec again: claim holds = CAUGHT, fails = SURVIVED (both summaries in the why). An expression is read as `return <expr>` when both sides are expressions (a leading `#` would otherwise read as a shebang, CART-1177). The rule generalizes carried operands — the claim says how many sites it mutated. `ground=1` applies EXACTLY the written text at its one site through the edit verb's classification (no rule is learned; a result that already occurs elsewhere refuses as drifted — widen the anchor): use it for any mutation inside a boolean chain or a comparison, where a learned rule mutates many unrelated sites and a SURVIVED describes a different mutant (MEASURED: an `or`-chain edit in xlang.lua learned as 4 rules at 10 sites SURVIVED; ground, it was CAUGHT). The working tree is never written. `spec-fails` (discovery) is the oracle alone: `run spec-fails - spec=<x>_spec [root=…]`. A mutant that makes the spec HANG (an infinite loop) is a FAILURE named `TIMED OUT` — the runner is its own process group and is killed whole at the timeout (before 2026-09-30 the tactic raised, and each hung `node` child spun on at 100% CPU). CART-1174 |\n| How much of these commits did CARTOGRAPH write (vs hand edits)? (the in-the-loop ratio) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run provenance-audit - [range=HEAD~20..HEAD] [repo=<dir>]` — every journal whose recorded root lies in the repo (journal.survey), each entry's ADDED lines matched as a multiset against each commit's `git show -U0` added lines (line level: a mixed commit splits honestly); per commit explained vs hand bytes, the overall ratio (reported, never a gate). Baseline 2026-09-28: 0.13% of the last 30 commits. Not yet: stale decisions / placeholders (the ledger, CART-1180). CART-1181 |\n| Which journal entries (and which DECISIONS) wrote this commit? Does the provenance travel with the repo? (the ledger) | Every journal entry records `decided_by` (`tactic` / `direct`) and `decisions = { { kind, key, by = accept-list \\| remembered, decision_id } }` (CART-1179). The LEDGER: one git note per commit on `refs/notes/cartograph` — `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/provledger.lua write [<rev>]` / `show [<rev>]`; `.githooks/post-commit` writes it after every commit (never fails a commit). In Lua: `require('cartograph.provenance').attribute(repo, range)` (the shared line-level attribution), `.ledger_row`, `.write_note`, `.read_note`. Notes travel only when pushed (`git push origin refs/notes/cartograph`). ★ The ledger only TIGHTENS: a note claiming a decision accepted answers nothing (pinned). An entry explains ONLY the commit its change landed in (CART-1195): made no later than the commit, not already in its parent, and — when older than the parent commit — none of it in the parent; before, 22 of 135 entries were credited to several commits. CART-1180 |\n| May this record LEAVE THE MACHINE? (a ledger note, a token, an invocation — does it carry an untracked file's bytes, a `.env`?) | `require('cartograph.sensitive')` — `classify(root, rels)` → `{ [rel] = why }`: DERIVED, a file git tracks is public, untracked/ignored is sensitive, a world with no repo is all sensitive; user `config.at(<abs>, 'sensitive') = true/false` overrides (never the tree). `reference(v)` = what travels in a sensitive value's place (`{ ref = 'sha256:…', sensitive = true }`). `leaks(record, images)` = the EGRESS check, independent of the redaction: any line (≥ 12 chars) of a sensitive file's images anywhere in the record's strings → `{ file, why }`, the bytes never named. Wired: every journal entry now records its INVOCATION (`{ verb, args, where }`, CART-1191 leaf 1 — local); the ledger note carries each entry's invocation with paths named `@<world id>/…`, or — when the entry touched a sensitive file — only `{ verb, args = reference, sensitive_files = n }`; `write_note` refuses a note `leaks` flags (class `decision`, nothing written). CART-1192 / CART-1193 |\n| Merge ANOTHER checkout's work into this one — without a line or syntax merge (their intents, replayed here) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/replay.lua <into dir> <from repo> <range> [apply=1] [approvals=<dir>]`, or OVER A SHARED REMOTE `tools/replay.lua <into dir> --remote <name> <range>` (the teammate pushed their branch AND `refs/notes/cartograph`; `replay.fetch` brings the notes into a MIRROR, `refs/notes/cartograph-remotes/<name>`, never merged into this repo's own; no git merge happens — their intents are replayed in your working tree) — reads the INVOCATIONS the ledger notes of `<range>` carry (journal entries record `{ verb, args }` since CART-1191 leaf 1) and re-runs them here in order through the tactic runner; each step classifies ITSELF by its verb's goal check: `done` (already here — the same change on both sides), `applied`, `conflict` (the verb refused it STALE: this world moved at the site — a decision, keep-mine / take-theirs), `frontier` (it did not travel: a sensitive step's args are a hash, its paths name another world, or the entry recorded no invocation). PAST A STOP a later step still runs when it shares NO touched file with any stopped step (the invocation records the plan's touched set; measured: a conflict left 92.3% of later steps independent); a step that shares one — or whose files are unknown (an older entry, a sensitive or cross-world one) — is `not reached` and blocks its own files in turn; `stop_at_first` restores stopping at the first stop. The note carries a REPLAY list: every applied step of the commit's window that touched a file it changed, superseded ones included (a step whose lines a later step overwrote explains nothing, yet the later one was planned against it). Disjoint work commutes (A+replay(B) = B+replay(A), pinned). A preview plans each step against the world AS IT IS; apply=1 is the merge (journaled, undoable). In Lua `require('cartograph.replay')` — `from_notes(repo, range)`, `run(store, items, opts)`, `localize(args, root)`. ⚠ An entry the attribution credits to several commits is replayed once (CART-1195). CART-1191 |\n| How DEPENDENT are replayed steps on each other — would a conflict block work that does not depend on it? | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/replaydeps.lua <repo> <range>` — replays the range's invocations into a scratch clone of its base, once in full and once per step LEFT OUT (each step as its own replay, so a stop is not read as a dependence); step j depends on i when j's outcome changes without i. Reports the dependent-pair rate, the share of later steps a conflict at i would leave INDEPENDENT, and the agreement of two derived proxies (same `file` arg; ANCHOR — j's `before` holds a line i's `after` added). MEASURED 2026-09-28 on 2a7991d..964881d (15 edit steps): 6 of 105 pairs dependent (5.7%); a conflict leaves 92.3% of later steps independent; both proxies missed 0, same-file 14 false+, anchor 4. ⚠ SLOW: 1960 s for 15 steps (a full extract per run, and steps before the left-out one are re-run needlessly). CART-1191 leaf 3 |\n\n\n\n| Is the LOSSLESS reader safe on this language / this tree? (may learned rules and term edits be written back into it) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/identity.lua <dir> <lang> [<lang> …]` — every file of the language (by `treesitter.parse_lang`), `cst_print(read(src)) == src` byte for byte; identical / REFUSED by reason (an error node: tree-sitter could not parse it) / PRINT MISMATCH (a real reader bug — must be 0). In Lua `require('cartograph.algebraread').identity(dir, lang)`, `.read(src, lang)`, `.escapes(lang)` (a grammar type that collides with an algebra kind, e.g. `pair` in js/ts/python/json/ruby, gets a DERIVED escaped kind `pair_`; printing is unaffected). Audited 2026-09-28, zero mismatches: javascript, typescript, python, ruby, java, go (100% identical on ghost / django-oscar / discourse / big-app.spring / amass); bash 92%, c 21%, cpp 74% (the rest refused, never mis-read). rewrite-by-example takes `lang` (files chosen by parse_lang) |\n| Edit a file (or create one) at ONE site, idempotently, through the journal — instead of sed / a python replace | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run edit - file=<rel> before=@<file> after=@<file> [apply=1]` (`-` = the current directory's world, no graph extracted; a dry run PREVIEWS). In a tactic: `T.step('edit', { file, before, after })`; in Lua `require('cartograph.edit').plan(store, { file, before, after })` / `.classify(text, before, after)`. `before` must occur EXACTLY ONCE (add context); `''` creates. IDEMPOTENT by classifying the current text against BOTH images: pending -> apply, done -> EMPTY (the re-run writes nothing), drifted -> refused by name (stale; a coincidental result elsewhere is drifted, never \"done\"); CONTAINMENT picks the witness (an append is judged by `after`, a delete by `before`). The `parses` guard is always declared and makes NO CLAIM for a path without a parser (markdown, shell). Floor of CART-1182: text on the graph's own world; term kinds, namespace addresses and non-disk commits are next |\n| Rename a record's FIELD (not a name): its reads and the constructors that define it, with an occupied target asked, not refused | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run rename-field <root> base=<spelling> field=<old> to=<new> define=<a.lua,b.lua> [accept=name-occupied] [apply=1]`; in a tactic `T.step('rename-field', { base, field, to, define })`. Reads = `<base>.<field>` (rooted by SPELLING — `spec.scopes`, never `L.scopes`; a record read under another name is the frontier); keys = `field =` in the define files (omitted: a DECISION listing every file with such a key). An OCCUPIED target (a read or a key already named `to`) is a `name-occupied` DECISION; accepted, the occupant moves to the placeholder `<to>__1` in the same plan and a `placeholder` residue says to choose its real name (CART-1009). Comment/string mentions: a counted `text-mentions` frontier, never rewritten. Replayed on CART-1160 step 7 (2203d17^): the same 3 reads + 3 keys, `binders` stopped as occupied. CART-1175 |\n| Deploy through a PLAN with review gates and a real undo (release, switch, health check; roll back on failure) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run deploy <root> from=<src dir> target=<deploy dir> [approve=yes] [rollback=1] [apply=1]` — the local deployment world (cartograph.deploy): `release` writes releases/<content id>/ (an immutable VALUE; a cross-world create journaled in the target's journal; the same content is the same id, so a re-release is empty), `switch` moves CURRENT (COMPENSABLE: its recorded inverse switches back, CART-1186; gated by the `approve-deploy` DECISION), then the `deploy-health` PREMISE (automated review). Gates: `target-write` + `approve-deploy` (approve=yes, or a remembered decision). rollback=1: a failed health check compensates the switch and undoes the release — the target byte-for-byte as it was. Compensation in general: a compensable verb's `compensate(args, entry)` returns the inverse INVOCATION; rollback runs it through the same step executor. CART-0154 / CART-1186 |\n| Compose write verbs toward a goal and stop ONLY on a genuine decision (a tactic: then / first / try / repeat / each) | `local tactic = require 'cartograph.tactic'; local T = tactic.T` → `tactic.run(store, T.seq(T.step('moveset', {seed_refs, dest}, {'surface'}), T.step('replace', {ref, text}, {'supplied-text'})), { apply = true, oracle = fn })` → `status` done / previewed / stopped (a decision, with `options` + `fixes`) / failed (class, where); `residue`, `work_orders` (every unbuilt stop), `rolled_back`. A DRY run (`apply = false`, the default) CHAINS OVERLAY WORLDS: each journaled step's staged texts become a virtual graph (`world.edit`) the next step plans in, so the whole term previews — `preview` = { [rel] = final text }, `worlds` = how many were stacked, the caller's graph comes back afterwards; a dry `repeat` converges on its own effect; a failed dry alternative drops its world; after a non-journaled step the rest is `underivable` (CART-1160 step 3). FORWARD RECOVERY: a run that stops KEEPS its completed steps (`completed`, each with its `effect`) and resuming is RE-RUNNING the same term with the decision answered in the step's `accept` list — every verb is goal-checked, so a completed step is `empty` the second time (`resumable`); `on_stop = 'rollback'` undoes a stopped run only over journaled writes. Verbs declare `effect` (journaled / compensable / irreversible; undeclared = irreversible, never inside first/try/repeat) and `rerun = 'empty'`. Verbs: `compose.VERBS` (moveset, replace, annotate, clonemerge), addressed by durable ref. One step alone: `tactic.step(store, {verb, args, accept}, {apply})` (compose's recipes run on it). CART-1152 |\n| Did you mean? — correct a refused invocation's argument (a slipped name, a wrong file, a symbol that MOVED or was RENAMED) | in a tactic: a plan-phase `ill-posed`/`stale` refusal asks `require('cartograph.correct').suggest(store, spec, args)` (the adapter declares `correct = { ref = 'ref' }` / `{ seed_refs = 'refs' }`) and keeps the candidates the VERB accepts (a dry step, no failing guard). ★ AUTOMATIC ONLY ON PROVENANCE: a stale ref to a symbol THIS run's own step moved (the adapter's `provides`; also on a resume, where the move is empty) is corrected, and listed in `result.corrections`. Everything INFERRED (edit distance, the witness — a SHAPE hash, CART-1154 —, the same name elsewhere, a same-shaped rename) is a DECISION whose options carry the corrected args (`source` moved|near|renamed|elsewhere, `CONTRADICTS` when the ref's witness disagrees). A witnessed ref whose symbol is GONE fails `stale` and lists ne