ith `{ share = { <exporter point> } }`; rows carry `peer` and `tier = 'xlang'`, a key two peers both export is an `ambiguous` miss. Gate against the MERGED graph: `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/peergate.lua <root> <exporter subdir> <importer subdir> <verb>:<arg>:<handler pattern>[:ident]` (luanti `src builtin API_FCT:1:l_%s:ident`: MATCH 290 · MISS 0 · EXTRA 0, 154 registrations = the textual count). CART-1160 step 9 |\n| Write a DIFFERENT world than the graph a plan was made against (promote into the built-in toolbelt, write a peer project) | `txn.target(plan, root, why)` → `plan.target = { root, why }` + a DECISION hazard naming the target (the builder's own when its evidence has `target = root`, else a generic `target-write`). The write needs the target MOUNTED WRITABLE: `txn.apply(store, plan, { ns })` where `txn.writable(ns, root)` finds an entry with `opts.rw`; without it the stage refuses as `decision`. In a tactic the grant is the ACCEPTED decision (`txn.grant(ns, plan, accepted)` mounts the target rw; `tactic.run(..., { ns })` supplies a base namespace). Containment is checked against the target root, the journal entry lives in the TARGET world's journal (`entry.root`; `tactic.rollback` undoes it there), the graph is not refreshed, and a dry run does not chain a cross-world step (the rest is underivable). Promotion uses it: `promote-tactic` with `into` outside the graph writes that world, `promote` being the one question. CART-1160 step 5 |\n| Answer a tactic's question ONCE and have later runs use the answer (a remembered decision) — for this exact question, or every question of one kind about files in a directory | A stopped run's `options[i].key` is the question's identity (kind + verb + evidence + touched files + target, hashed; the prose is not in it). `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/decisions.lua remember kind=<k> key=<key> [why=…]` (exact) · `remember kind=<k> dir=<dir>` / `file=<f>` (a VIEW: every `<k>` decision whose touched files ALL lie inside it; views are namespace mounts, the most specific leads and every covering view is cited) · `list` · `forget <id>`. In Lua: `require('cartograph.decisions').remember/forget/list/lookup`. `tactic.step` takes a covered decision as accepted — a remembered `target-write` is a grant — and the residue says who answered (`remembered`, `decision_id`); `tactic.run(..., { remembered = false })` asks everything again. The record is the USER's (the state dir); never written by a run, never inside the tree, and NO MCP verb writes it (toolbelt_run shows the keys). CART-1160 step 6 |\n| Let a TEAMMATE answer a tactic's question from their own checkout (a signed approval that travels; who may answer which kind) | A stopped run's options carry `portable_key` — the question named by each world's DERIVED identity (git origin + prefix), so it is the same from any checkout of that origin (a world with no origin says `portable_why` instead). The teammate signs it: `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/approvals.lua sign <private key> <principal> <kind> <portable key> <out dir> [why]` (ssh-keygen, namespace `cartograph-decision` — a git signature never replays as an approval); `list <dir>` shows each token VERIFIED or why not; `roster` = where YOUR ssh `allowed_signers` lives (state dir; never read from the tree — no roster, no signed answer). Run with the store: `tools/toolbelt.lua run … approvals=<dir>` / `tactic.run(store, term, { approvals = dir | {tokens} })`. A token answers only when its signature verifies AND config `deciders = { [kind] = { 'alice@team', 'role:reviewers' } }` (+ `roles`), looked up per file with `config.at`, routes that kind for EVERY subject to the signer; every token found that did not answer is named in the option's `refused_approvals`. The residue says `approved by <principal>`; the journal entry records `by = 'signed'`, the principal and the token id (so the ledger carries it). In Lua `require 'cartograph.approvals'` (`portable_key`, `world_id`, `sign`, `verify`, `authorized`, `load`, `write`). Signing has no MCP verb. CART-1183 |\n| Which setting applies HERE, and which entry decided it? (scoped configuration by subject — a dir, a file, a query) | `require('cartograph.config').at(subject_abs_path, key)` → `value, { source = 'scoped', scope }` / `{ source = 'global' }` / `nil, { source = 'ambiguous', entries }`. Scopes (user-side `setup{ scoped = … }`): map form `['/abs/dir' or '/abs/file'] = { key = v }`, list form `{ dir = … | file = … | query = fn(path) -> bool, name, values = { … } }`. The most specific containing scope wins (a subset beats its superset); a QUERY's containment is EXTENSIONAL over the loaded graph's files (unknown without one); most-specific scopes that disagree — or one scope given two values — are AMBIGUOUS, never picked. `config.explain(subject)` = every scoped key's effective value + provenance; `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/config.lua at <path> [key]`. `for_root(root, key)` is its dir case. Read through it today: behaviour_suppliers, tactic_overrides, `exclude` (per directory walked), `entrypoints` (a real disagreement classifies the file `ambiguous`, never `orphan`). CART-1120 / CART-1160 step 6 |\n| Work on a tree whose full graph does not fit (or need not): hold only a SKELETON and fetch detail on demand, bounded | Ingest the thin index (`ts.index_only(root)`): `store.ingest` installs GENERATED ADAPTERS (`cartograph.adapters`) — reading a detail field (`n.df`, `n.flow`, so `df.stmts(n)`) fills that node's FILE on demand instead of answering nil/{}. The adapters are DERIVED: `adapters.derive()` probes each producer on a tiny skeleton, records the fields it wrote and whether it stayed LOCAL (no graph-level change); only local producers (dataflow) trigger on a read, a non-local one (the call relink) runs only via `adapters.prefetch(store, files, fields)`, which picks the cheapest producer per field. `adapters.budget = N` bounds resident files (LRU eviction; re-read re-fills); `adapters.resident(store)`, `adapters.stats`. Per graph (`data._adapters`); a node fills only while its graph is the lens. Measured (`nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/skeletonrss.lua [root] [budget]`): lua/cartograph, 302 files / 5691 fns — full 70.0 MB heap vs skeleton + adapters 12.3 MB at budget 8, 0 rows differ. ⚠ a presence probe must use `rawget` on a skeleton (a plain read fills). CART-1160 step 8 |\n| What would the graph look like AFTER these edits — without writing them? (an overlay world) | `require('cartograph.world').edit(base_data, { [rel] = text })` -> a VIRTUAL graph: a shallow copy of the base with every node/edge/call record copied, an `overlay` transport layer serving the edited texts first, spliced on the copy (edges INTO an edited file from unchanged callers re-derived). Query it with `store.scoped(over, fn)`; edits STACK (an edit on an overlay keeps the lower texts). The base graph and the disk are untouched (world_spec fingerprints the whole base); planners read the overlay through `txn.read_file`, and `store.content` / `store.stale` / the frontier-text read go through the graph's transport too (an overlay's lines, readfile's semantics); a dry `tactic.run` / `compose.run` chains these worlds step by step; `txn.apply` REFUSES a virtual graph. Acceptance: the overlay equals a rebuild of the edited world, row by row. Transport kind: `{ kind = 'overlay', files = { [abs] = text } }`. CART-1160 step 3 |\n| Run something against a SCRATCH graph without losing the one I have loaded (an example, a fixture, a probe) | `require('cartograph.store').scoped(extraction, fn)` — fn runs with the scratch graph as the lens; the caller's graph (data, generation, range columns) comes back afterwards, raise or not; nothing is re-extracted. Toolbelt examples always run this way. Anything graph-derived held OUTSIDE the store's fields registers in `store.LENS_HOOKS` (fn(data) after every restore) — `at`'s coordinate columns are the first (CART-1159). The arc: CART-1160 (mounts for graphs) |\n| Is there a named tactic for this? Re-measure a recorded discovery — does the finding still hold? | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua list` (every entry, with its examples as usage) · `run <name> <dir> [key=value ...]` (a discovery re-measures the tree; a write tactic PREVIEWS, `apply=1` writes) · `examples [name]` (run them: usage AND test). In process: `require('cartograph.toolbelt').run(store, name, params, opts)`. MCP: `toolbelt_list`, `toolbelt_run { name, params, apply? }` (a write PREVIEWS unless `apply` on a writable host — the whole tactic, `preview` = a diff per file, `worlds`). COMPOSE by name inside a tactic: `T.use(name, params)` — a write entry runs its term, a discovery is a PREMISE gate (fails ill-posed when its claim does not hold); a cycle of uses refuses. Params are coerced by the entry's declaration everywhere (`toolbelt.coerce`): a ref may be `file::name` (did-you-mean on a miss), a string `@path`, a list `a,b`; an undeclared or missing param refuses by name. Entries are plain Lua files in `lua/cartograph/tactics/` — no central list; kinds `write` (build(params) -> a tactic term) and `discovery` (measure + claim, `measures` = the ticket it tracks); each carries `examples` the fence runs (tests/toolbelt_spec.lua). The catalogue is `list` — derived from the directory (a hand list here had drifted to 8 of 15 entries). An example may declare `requires = function () -> ok, why end` (a compiler, a parser): unmet, it SKIPS and the fence counts it as a skip, never a pass |\n| Does this spec actually CATCH a regression in this guard? (a mutation check — never sed the working tree) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run mutation-check - file=<rel> before='<expr or chunk>' after='<mutated>' spec=<name>_spec [repo=<dir>] [keep=1]` — copies the repo (all but .git) to a scratch root, runs the spec there FIRST (a red baseline or a run of nothing REFUSES), applies the mutation by example (rewrite-by-example: staged, parse-guarded; a mutation that matches nothing REFUSES by name), runs the spec again: claim holds = CAUGHT, fails = SURVIVED (both summaries in the why). An expression is read as `return <expr>` when both sides are expressions (a leading `#` would otherwise read as a shebang, CART-1177). The rule generalizes carried operands — the claim says how many sites it mutated. `ground=1` applies EXACTLY the written text at its one site through the edit verb's classification (no rule is learned; a result that already occurs elsewhere refuses as drifted — widen the anchor): use it for any mutation inside a boolean chain or a comparison, where a learned rule mutates many unrelated sites and a SURVIVED describes a different mutant (MEASURED: an `or`-chain edit in xlang.lua learned as 4 rules at 10 sites SURVIVED; ground, it was CAUGHT). The working tree is never written. `spec-fails` (discovery) is the oracle alone: `run spec-fails - spec=<x>_spec [root=…]`. A mutant that makes the spec HANG (an infinite loop) is a FAILURE named `TIMED OUT` — the runner is its own process group and is killed whole at the timeout (before 2026-09-30 the tactic raised, and each hung `node` child spun on at 100% CPU). CART-1174 |\n| How much of these commits did CARTOGRAPH write (vs hand edits)? (the in-the-loop ratio) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run provenance-audit - [range=HEAD~20..HEAD] [repo=<dir>]` — every journal whose recorded root lies in the repo (journal.survey), each entry's ADDED lines matched as a multiset against each commit's `git show -U0` added lines (line level: a mixed commit splits honestly); per commit explained vs hand bytes, the overall ratio (reported, never a gate). Baseline 2026-09-28: 0.13% of the last 30 commits. Not yet: stale decisions / placeholders (the ledger, CART-1180). CART-1181 |\n| Which journal entries (and which DECISIONS) wrote this commit? Does the provenance travel with the repo? (the ledger) | Every journal entry records `decided_by` (`tactic