e's fields registers in `store.LENS_HOOKS` (fn(data) after every restore) — `at`'s coordinate columns are the first (CART-1159). The arc: CART-1160 (mounts for graphs) |\n| Is there a named tactic for this? Re-measure a recorded discovery — does the finding still hold? | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua list` (every entry, with its examples as usage) · `run <name> <dir> [key=value ...]` (a discovery re-measures the tree; a write tactic PREVIEWS, `apply=1` writes) · `examples [name]` (run them: usage AND test). In process: `require('cartograph.toolbelt').run(store, name, params, opts)`. MCP: `toolbelt_list`, `toolbelt_run { name, params, apply? }` (a write PREVIEWS unless `apply` on a writable host — the whole tactic, `preview` = a diff per file, `worlds`). COMPOSE by name inside a tactic: `T.use(name, params)` — a write entry runs its term, a discovery is a PREMISE gate (fails ill-posed when its claim does not hold); a cycle of uses refuses. Params are coerced by the entry's declaration everywhere (`toolbelt.coerce`): a ref may be `file::name` (did-you-mean on a miss), a string `@path`, a list `a,b`; an undeclared or missing param refuses by name. Entries are plain Lua files in `lua/cartograph/tactics/` — no central list; kinds `write` (build(params) -> a tactic term) and `discovery` (measure + claim, `measures` = the ticket it tracks); each carries `examples` the fence runs (tests/toolbelt_spec.lua). The catalogue is `list` — derived from the directory (a hand list here had drifted to 8 of 15 entries). An example may declare `requires = function () -> ok, why end` (a compiler, a parser): unmet, it SKIPS and the fence counts it as a skip, never a pass |\n| Does this spec actually CATCH a regression in this guard? (a mutation check — never sed the working tree) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run mutation-check - file=<rel> before='<expr or chunk>' after='<mutated>' spec=<name>_spec [repo=<dir>] [keep=1]` — copies the repo (all but .git